    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d220-a1">

                <pb facs="0375" n="263" sameAs="frus:frus1907p1/pg263"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure 1.]</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Chargé Fletcher to Consul-General Denby</hi>.</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >American Legation</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Peking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian" when="1907-10-01"
                                ><hi rendition="#italic">October 1,
                    1907</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: The legation is in receipt of your No. 17
                    of the 25th ultimo, transmitting the text of a proclamation of the taotai of
                    Shanghai relative to the protection of American trade-marks, and notes that you
                    believe this proclamation to be couched in satisfactory language. It is rather
                    unfortunate that reference was made in the proclamation to any particular brand
                    of merchandise, as it detracts somewhat from the force of the general
                    prohibition.</p>
                <p>I am, etc.,</p>
                <closer>(Signed) <signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Henry P.
                        Fletcher.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
